@node Getting Started
@chapter Connecting Infix to a Flake
@cindex flake inputs
@cindex flake-parts
@vindex flakeModules.configurations
@vindex flakeModules.devshell

  A @dfn{flake module} extends the options available to a flake-parts
configuration.  Importing an infix module makes its options available;
you then declare the homes, systems, or shells you need.  These modules
are separate from Nixpkgs overlays.  You do not need an infix overlay to
use them.

  The following @file{flake.nix} connects both modules and their inputs.
It leaves the configuration declarations to the examples in the
following chapters.  Keep only the imports and inputs needed by your
project.

@example
@{
  inputs = @{
    devshell = @{
      inputs = @{
        nixpkgs = @{
          follows = "nixpkgs";
        @};
      @};

      url = "github:numtide/devshell";
    @};

    flake-parts = @{
      inputs = @{
        nixpkgs-lib = @{
          follows = "nixpkgs";
        @};
      @};

      url = "github:hercules-ci/flake-parts";
    @};

    home-manager = @{
      inputs = @{
        nixpkgs = @{
          follows = "nixpkgs";
        @};
      @};

      url = "github:nix-community/home-manager";
    @};

    infix = @{
      inputs = @{
        nixpkgs = @{
          follows = "nixpkgs";
        @};
      @};

      url = "git+https://github.com/brsvh/infix.git";
    @};

    nixpkgs = @{
      url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixpkgs-unstable";
    @};
  @};

  outputs =
    inputs@@@{
      flake-parts,
      infix,
      ...
    @}:
    flake-parts.lib.mkFlake
      @{
        inherit
          inputs
          ;
      @}
      @{
        imports = [
          infix.flakeModules.configurations
          infix.flakeModules.devshell
        ];

        systems = [
          "x86_64-linux"
        ];
      @};
@}
@end example

@noindent
The @code{follows} declarations share the chosen Nixpkgs input.  Choose
compatible revisions of Nixpkgs, Home Manager, and devshell for your
project; the example uses their development branches.

  @code{flakeModules.configurations} uses the inputs named
@code{nixpkgs} and @code{home-manager}.  It produces named configuration
outputs independently of the flake-parts @code{systems} list.
@code{flakeModules.devshell} uses the input named @code{devshell} and
the per-system @code{pkgs} argument.  List the platforms for which you
want shell outputs in @code{systems}.

  To use a later example, place its module in a file and add that path
to @code{imports}, alongside the infix modules.  Paths such as
@file{./home/alice.nix} in the examples refer to files in your project;
replace them with your own modules.  Infix does not discover modules or
profiles by scanning project directories.

  Home Manager and devshell retain their upstream options.  Consult the
@url{https://nix-community.github.io/home-manager/, Home Manager manual}
and the @url{https://numtide.github.io/devshell/, devshell
documentation} for the settings of those applications.  The following
chapters describe infix's declarations and how it combines them.

